WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.185 --> 00:00:03.353
(funk music)

00:00:03.353 --> 00:00:06.020
- So there's a new ritual that
a lots of us share right now.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.640
Checking our temperature
to see if we have a fever.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.720
And it's understandable.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.540
Fever's are common symptoms of COVID-19.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.920
But if you're hoping your
temperature is a nice 98.6 degrees

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.890
or 37 Celsius.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.140
You might learn something
new about yourself.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:22.853
- 97.25.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:24.543
- 98.2.

00:00:25.565 --> 00:00:26.593
- 97.1.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:28.483
- Very low,

00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:29.853
Am I OK?

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.330
It turns out 98.6 degrees
fahrenheit isn't really the

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.810
ideal or normal body temperature.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.470
There's really no such thing.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.090
But temperature can still tell
us a lot about our health.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.983
And about the spread of COVID-19.

00:00:44.303 --> 00:00:46.700
(temperature machines beeping)

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.010
The number 98.6 mostly came
from 19th century German

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.700
researcher Carl Reinhold
August Wunderlich.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.910
He claims to catch the
numbers of more than a million

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.080
in temperature readings
to thousands of patients.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.950
And he arrived at the number of 98.6.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.080
That number has stuck around ever since.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.440
But person to person

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.220
it's kinda meaningless.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.480
We asked our co workers
to take their own readings

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.613
and this is what normal
temperature looks like for us.

00:01:11.876 --> 00:01:12.709
- 95.83.

00:01:14.959 --> 00:01:16.980
- 96.7 which seems low.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.250
- I'm a cold,

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.210
cold man.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.240
- Maybe [Inaudible]

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.550
I would have thought.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:23.760
- It's fine.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.360
I guess.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.570
- Wunderlich himself knew
that body temperature varied.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.410
He recorded a healthy
range from 97 and a quarter

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.880
to 99 and a half in his subjects.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.870
Other studies have shown
that temperature can differ

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:38.100
noticeably across age,
gender, size and race.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.940
And modern research points
to a much lower number.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.290
Around 98.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:45.000
That squares with our very
informal staff average.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.940
We're around 97.5.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.212
- You let her write the
thermometers broken.

00:01:49.212 --> 00:01:50.045
I don't know.

00:01:50.045 --> 00:01:52.690
- What's more, you yourself
don't have one body temperature

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.040
you have a range.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.210
Our team all took their ratings at 10am.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.680
But if you took yours a bunch
over the course of a day,

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.250
you'll see it change.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.010
Most people are coolest in the morning

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.950
and warmest in the early evening.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.190
It's a reflection of your
bodies natural rhythms,

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:10.020
your digestion, hormone
production and cognition and more.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.450
Here's what my temperature looks like

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.030
throughout the day.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.680
- Other bodily cycles like
ovulation can cause small

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.750
swings in temperature too
and different parts of you

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.310
are different temperatures.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.700
Under the tongue, armpit,
forehead and rectal readings

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.720
are all likely to be unique.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.740
Point is there are tons of variables.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.970
We could be many temperatures
all at once all the time

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.793
and still be perfectly healthy.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.485
- But being sick is another story.

00:02:36.485 --> 00:02:39.290
(theme sound)

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.660
So, I spoke with a couple
of people who are connecting

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:42.930
some interesting dots

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.050
between temperature variation

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.350
and an illness like COVID-19.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:48.280
When you get sick,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.880
many of your bodies
natural predictable rhythms

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.050
start to destabilize.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.920
Your temperature might
fluctuate more radically

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.870
and it might crank up
above your natural range.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.330
That's your body triggering
a fever to make itself

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.320
less hospitable to viruses or bacteria.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.160
In other words

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.550
your body temperature gets anomalous.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.200
That's trouble for you,

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.070
but for people tracking COVID,

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.700
it's crucial data.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.540
One effort is Kinsa.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.220
A company that tracks the
spread of infectious disease

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.080
using fevers.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.850
Kinsa sells a smart
thermometer that sends your

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:24.900
temperature back to the
company every time you take it.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.260
Kinsa says they have about
a million thermometers

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.500
in circulation.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.460
And with all that data,

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.200
they've built a real
time health weather map.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.730
It shows possible hot spots of COVID,

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:38.880
counties with more fever then
flu season would explain.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.710
- Let's take our real time signal

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:44.480
remove the expected value cold and flu.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.860
What's left over is residual.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.110
That residual is a
typical, in this particular

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.680
analysis a typical fever clusters.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.602
Things you would not expect.

00:03:54.602 --> 00:03:57.830
What we found since that point is that

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.610
there's a very high correlation
between our hot spots

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.063
and COVID-19 cases.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.420
The work is not peer reviewed yet.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.020
And some experts question
how reliable it can be

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.710
with only a million thermometers.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.710
Their user space could also be biased

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.300
by race or class or other factors.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:16.350
But Kinsa still thinks it can tease out

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.870
some interesting trends,

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:19.860
like the effect of social distancing.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.080
Here's their look at Miami-Dade County

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.370
in the middle of March.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.433
In 9 days,

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.100
the percent of people with a fever

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.220
more than doubled.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:31.970
And around the 17th and 18th,

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.500
they started implementing
the closure bars.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.763
So it starts going down.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.780
- Other researchers are
taking a different attack.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.350
Looking for clues in body
temperature that might

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.470
predict a fever before it starts.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.320
- One study out of UCFS
would collect temperature

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.740
and other health data from
participants by using a

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.000
smart ring.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.620
The rings can measure small
changes in body temperature

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:55.860
every minute.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.800
The question is, can
tiny temperature rhythms

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.187
predict a brewing infections like COVID.

00:05:01.187 --> 00:05:03.254
- And so we could pick
up with great reliability

00:05:03.254 --> 00:05:07.330
those ultranium rhythms,
those within a day rhythms,

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:09.900
that there are frequency
that tend to tell us about

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.410
how stagily your hormones
passing along, how synchronized

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.060
are they inside of you.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:16.770
- It's early days for the study

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.420
but once smart ring user

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.620
who tested positive for
COVID has already shared

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:22.600
some interesting data.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.660
- The temperature rhythms
that I receive within the day

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.086
start to sort of destabilize and amplify

00:05:29.086 --> 00:05:30.510
before they have a fever

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.380
right up to about 5 days beforehand.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.001
The whole system would look like

00:05:34.001 --> 00:05:36.300
it was sort of increasingly
like a teeter totter

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.330
before they have a fever,

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.330
before they felt sick.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.270
- Again that's just one patient.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.780
But with enough data,

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.740
the team hopes to build an algorithm

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.080
that can sniff out COVID
via rhythms like these.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.100
- We will be publishing the findings

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.980
we will be putting out, you know,

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:56.217
competition or calls for [Inaudible]

00:05:56.217 --> 00:05:59.020
get everybody in the room

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.550
let's get this as good as
we could possibly get it.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:03.260
This is really just

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.130
how big of a defense can we make.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:08.613
How quickly to get this into service.

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.760
- In the coming months while
we take our temperatures

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.290
every minute like maniacs

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.210
we're going to be hearing
a lot more about the effort

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.670
to track and predict COVID-19.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.620
Scientists will learn more
about how the human body

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.090
regulates itself and respond to disease.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.340
And along the way,

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.180
we might let go of some
assumptions about what's normal

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:27.270
and healthy.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.400
- Obsessing about 98.6 is
really false precision right?

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.490
Everybody should have a
nice daily rhythm of their

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:33.880
temperature.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.040
The only time your temperature
is constant is when your dead

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:37.640
and it's room temperature so

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:39.990
thinking about

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:43.190
the patterns of change rather
than the absolute number

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:44.340
is much more important.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:47.653
- Psst cat, go on.

00:06:48.797 --> 00:06:50.914
Oh cat get outta there.

00:06:50.914 --> 00:06:52.840
Cat [Inaudible]

